/*
 * HTJ2K channel-map probe for the OpenEXRCore public read API.
 *
 * The embedded image is an ordinary 6-channel, HTJ2K256-compressed
 * scanline-part OpenEXR file written with the public write API.  The only
 * difference from a well-formed file is one 16-bit field: the first chunk's
 * HTJ2K header maps its last codestream component to file channel 30000
 * instead of channel 5.  Everything else about the file is in contract --
 * valid magic, version, header, attributes, offset table, chunk sizes.
 *
 * Decoding the first scanline chunk through exr_decoding_run() must reject
 * that channel map as corrupt.  If the out-of-range channel index instead
 * reaches the offset computation in the HTJ2K decoder, the per-chunk
 * channel array is read far past its end and AddressSanitizer aborts
 * inside libOpenEXRCore.
 *
 * Build (C or C++, link a C++ driver if libOpenEXRCore was built static):
 *   cc -g -fsanitize=address -fno-omit-frame-pointer \
 *      -I<prefix>/include/OpenEXR probe.c -o probe \
 *      <prefix>/lib/libOpenEXRCore-*.a -lpthread -ldl -lm
 */
